{"graph_chars": "Chapter Two's Bring It Home returns to the degree. Some people do not hold a bachelor's because tuition is high. Some do not because the full opportunity cost, including years of lost earnings, is too high. That choice lives inside their opportunity set. Before you go, here is the recitation for both chapters. Economics studies how humans make decisions in the face of scarcity. Scarcity is wants exceeding available supply. Time is the ultimate scarce resource. Division of labor splits production into tasks. Specialization raises output for three reasons: matching advantage, learning and quality, and economies of scale. Specialization requires trade. Micro looks at households, workers, and firms. Macro looks at growth, unemployment, and inflation. This course is macro. The circular flow has households and firms in a product market and a labor market. Economies are traditional, command, market, or mixed. Globalization is trade plus financial capital flows. Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative. Alphonso's ten dollars buy five burgers or twenty tickets. A burger costs four tickets. Utility diminishes on the margin. Ignore sunk costs. The P P F is curved because opportunity cost rises. Productive efficiency is the frontier. Allocative efficiency is the mix society wants. Comparative advantage is lower opportunity cost. Positive describes. Normative prescribes. The invisible hand is self-interest that can serve the public. That is Chapters One and Two.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0638], [0.0638, 0.1276], [0.1276, 0.1913], [0.1913, 0.2551], [0.2551, 0.3189], [0.3189, 0.3827], [0.3827, 0.4465], [0.4465, 0.5102], [0.5102, 0.574], [0.574, 0.6378], [0.6378, 0.7016], [0.7016, 0.7654], [0.7654, 0.8291], [0.8291, 0.8929], [0.8929, 0.9567], [0.9567, 1.0205], [1.0205, 1.0843], [1.0843, 1.148], [1.148, 1.2118], [1.2118, 1.2756], [1.2756, 1.3394], [1.3394, 1.4032], [1.4032, 1.4669], [1.4669, 1.5307], [1.5307, 1.5945], [1.5945, 1.6583], [1.6583, 1.